     "type": "concept",
     "title": "The Stoic Lineage: Six Names to Know",
     "content": "Stoicism evolved across **five centuries** through six major figures. Knowing this lineage helps you read Marcus — he is constantly in dialogue with predecessors.\n\n• **Zeno of Citium** (334-262 BCE) — The founder. Shipwrecked merchant turned philosopher in Athens.\n• **Cleanthes** (330-230 BCE) — Zeno's successor. Water-carrier by night, philosopher by day. Wrote the **Hymn to Zeus**.\n• **Chrysippus** (279-206 BCE) — \"The second founder.\" Systematized Stoicism into 705 books (only fragments survive). \"Without Chrysippus, no Stoa.\"\n• **Seneca** (4 BCE-65 CE) — Roman statesman, Nero's tutor, playwright. Wrote **Letters to Lucilius** and essays like *On the Shortness of Life*.\n• **Musonius Rufus** (~30-100 CE) — \"The Roman Socrates.\" Taught both men and women. Mentor to Epictetus.\n• **Epictetus** (~50-135 CE) — Born a slave, became the most influential Stoic teacher. His student Arrian recorded his **Discourses** and **Enchiridion** (Handbook).\n• **Marcus Aurelius** (121-180 CE) — The final great Stoic. Read Epictetus throughout his reign.",
