     "type": "example",
     "title": "How Marcus Actually Died",
     "content": "In March 180 CE, at age 58, Marcus fell terminally ill at his military headquarters near **Vindobona** (modern Vienna) — likely plague, possibly aggravated by years of campaigning. The accounts of his final days (Cassius Dio, the Historia Augusta) read like a man taking his own course's final exam:\n\n• **He stopped eating and drinking**, accepting the end rather than clinging — consistent with the Stoic view that death at nature's signal is not to be fought with frenzy.\n\n• **He worried about his son, not himself.** His documented anxiety was for the empire under Commodus — a duty-concern, not a self-concern. (History vindicated the worry: Commodus became one of Rome's worst emperors. More on that sober lesson in the capstone.)\n\n• **He comforted his mourners** rather than the reverse. Dio reports him asking his weeping friends why they cried for him instead of thinking about the plague and the shared mortality of all: *\"Why do you weep for me, instead of thinking about the pestilence and about death which is the common lot of us all?\"*\n\n• **His reported last words**, to the tribune asking for the day's watchword: *\"Go to the rising sun; I am already setting.\"*\n\n• On the seventh day, he **covered his head as if to sleep**, and died in the night.\n\nFor decades he had written rehearsals for this exact event — 2.11, 7.69, the whole apparatus of memento mori. The historical testimony, even allowing for flattering sources, suggests **the practice held under final load**. That's the strongest evidence the Meditations offers: it was written by a man who eventually had to use it, and it worked.",
