     "type": "practice",
     "title": "Practice: The Virtue Audit",
     "content": "**Score yourself on the four cardinals, find the limiting virtue, and train it for a week. 30 minutes to set up.**\n\n1. **Rate honestly, with evidence** (15 min). For each virtue, recall the last two weeks and write 2-3 sentences of EVIDENCE before assigning 1-10. No evidence, no score:\n   • **WISDOM**: Did I examine impressions before believing them? Did I change my mind when shown wrong (6.21), or defend errors? Did I confuse indifferents with goods — treating a criticism as a catastrophe, a purchase as a salvation?\n   • **JUSTICE**: Did I keep every agreement? How did I treat people who could do nothing for me? Did I do anything FOR the common good, or merely avoid harming it?\n   • **COURAGE**: What truth did I avoid saying, and to whom? What right action did fear postpone? Did I endure the hard necessary things or negotiate exits?\n   • **TEMPERANCE**: Where did appetite outrank intention — food, phone, spending, outrage? Could I stop mid-pleasure when stopping was right? Any \"dye\" soaking in (6.30) — flattery, comfort, status?\n\n2. **Identify the limiting virtue** (5 min). The unity thesis says your LOWEST score is silently corrupting the others — courage deficits make justice flaky; temperance deficits cloud wisdom. Circle the lowest. That's the training target. (Resist training your favorite — that's the comfortable one precisely because it's already strong.)\n\n3. **Design one daily rep** (10 min). One small, repeatable act per day for seven days, targeting the circled virtue:\n   • Wisdom: one impression per day written down and examined before assent\n   • Justice: one daily act of unowed service, done without mention (the vine, 5.6)\n   • Courage: one avoided conversation or task per day, done before noon\n   • Temperance: one chosen renunciation per day — a skipped indulgence, a phone-free meal — held cheerfully, not grimly\n\n4. **Re-audit in seven days.** Same questions, fresh evidence. Marcus's Book 1 is precisely this exercise pointed backward — an inventory of virtues observed in others and debts incurred. Yours points forward.",
