     "explanation": "The architecture distributes the three disciplines across the day's natural joints. Morning is the rehearsal station: 2.1 pre-loads the day's friction ('I shall meet with the ungrateful...') with the kinship clause ready, while 5.1 wins the bed negotiation via the ergon argument — the day is met scripted. Daytime is the field station: prosochē (attention) runs the discipline of assent live — objective description on contact (3.11), one action at a time 'as if it were the last' (6.26, 8.32), micro-retreats into one's own soul (4.3) instead of waiting for vacations. Evening is the audit station: Seneca's three questions scan the day against virtue — not productivity — with the crucial tonal balance of prosecutor and pardoner ('this time I pardon you'). Note what the wrong options reveal: Stoicism never counsels avoiding difficult people (they're the gym equipment), never measures days in tasks completed, and is the opposite of thought-emptying — it's thought-EXAMINING, all day, on a schedule.",
