     "explanation": "Knowing the map matters practically — it tells you where to go next. Pigliucci (philosopher of science, CUNY) anchors the academic wing: doctrinal precision, updated physics, honest engagement with objections like the is-ought problem from Lesson 4. Robertson (CBT psychotherapist) anchors the therapeutic wing: his work demonstrates the assent-discipline → cognitive-restructuring lineage in clinical detail. Holiday anchors the popular wing — the largest audience and the most criticism, both deserved in parts. Irvine preceded them all (2009) and contributed the genuinely useful trichotomy: for partially-controllable outcomes like competitions, internalize the goal (play your best) rather than the result (win). The Modern Stoicism organization supplies what no ancient school had: annual psychometric data, via Stoic Week, showing modest but directionally stable improvements in flourishing measures (LeBon's analyses). The deeper point of the question: modern Stoicism is a quarrelsome school, not a church — disagreement among the wings is evidence of health, and your job as a student is to enter the argument, not to pick a pope.",
