     "explanation": "The relapse IS the curriculum arriving. Option A commits the sorting error the whole course targets: it treats a lapse (an event, now in the unchangeable past — Lesson 5) as a verdict on identity, and 'Stoicism failed' after twelve days is like declaring fitness a scam after two gym sessions. Option B misunderstands the practice's unit: reps are daily encounters with TODAY's judgments — yesterday's missed pages can't be done today because yesterday's mind is gone; doubling sessions services the streak (reputation-anxiety pointed inward, an indifferent), not the training. Option D inverts the sage doctrine: the sage's nonexistence is precisely why restart-from-zero perfectionism is incoherent — there is no flawless standard you fell from, only a progress line you rejoin. The correct protocol assembles the sources: 5.9's 'return again' without disgust, Seneca's pardon-with-resolution, and the diagnostic mining that converts the lapse into tomorrow's premeditatio material (the obstacle becomes the way — Lesson 8, applied to practice itself). Marcus filled twelve books with exactly these returns. The Meditations isn't the diary of a man who didn't relapse; it's the proof that returning, ten thousand times, is what the practice IS.",
